# Normalise line endings so the repository looks the same everywhere.
# Without this, git on Windows may rewrite every file on checkout and the
# first `git status` shows the whole project as modified.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files genuinely need CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# So does the licence NSIS shows before installing: its own documentation says
# the file must be in DOS text format. It also carries a UTF-8 BOM, which is
# how a Unicode makensis knows not to read it in the system code page — the
# bytes are content, so git leaves them alone either way.
src-tauri/LICENSE.txt text eol=crlf

# The NSIS language file is UTF-8 with NO byte-order mark, and that is not a
# preference — it is what the bundler requires, established by reading the
# bytes rather than by reasoning about them.
# Tauri does not copy this file, it reads it and writes it out with a UTF-8 BOM
# of its own. A source that carries one ends up with two (makensis: `Invalid
# command: "<BOM>;"`); a UTF-16 source ends up with a UTF-8 BOM in front of a
# UTF-16 one (`Bad text encoding`). Tauri's own generated English.nsh begins
# EF BB BF for exactly this reason — the mark is added at copy time and is not
# in its source either.
*.nsh text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch.
# NSIS reads these as 24-bit BMP. Without this line the blanket `* text=auto`
# above would treat them as text and rewrite bytes that look like line endings.
*.bmp binary
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.woff2 binary
*.bin binary
*.onnx binary
